// PARAM: --analysis interval
// "0 < y" holds on the then-branch: x is 5, so the second disjunct
// "(x < 0) == 1" is false in every state, and the branch is taken only when the
// first disjunct is true.
//
// Refining a disjunction joins both sides, so the analyzer sees it only because
// each side is gated first: bfilter's "Or _ _ True" case narrows each disjunct
// with the same feasibility test the branch transfer applies to a whole
// condition, and an infeasible side then contributes bot -- the unit of the
// join -- instead of its own unrefined incoming state. Without that gate the
// second disjunct would contribute y unconstrained and the join would discard
// what the first disjunct established. The branch transfer's own gate cannot
// substitute for it: it reads the whole condition, whose abstract value is
// [0,1], and so decides nothing.
//
// Example_Guard_Refinement.thy pins the same result on the abstract state
// (bfilter_or_drops_infeasible_disjunct) and states the join identity
// (bfilter_or_is_the_gated_join).
void main() {
  x := 5;
  y := __voblint_nondet_int();
  if (0 < y || (x < 0) == 1) {
    __voblint_check(0 < y) // PROVED
  } else {
    __voblint_check(true) // PROVED
  }
}
